Looking at this issue, the problem is that when handling HEAD requests, the `responseWriter.Close()` method calls `init()` which initializes the encoder and then closes it. Closing the encoder writes empty-stream trailers (20 bytes for gzip, 13 bytes for zstd), which net/http converts to `Content-Length` headers in HEAD responses.

The fix is to skip closing the encoder if no body was actually written. I need to track whether any data was written and only close/reset the encoder if data was written.

Let me trace through the code:
1. In `Close()`, if `!rw.wroteHeader`, it calls `rw.init()` which sets up the encoder
2. Then it closes the encoder, which flushes trailers
3. For HEAD requests, no body is written, so these trailers become the Content-Length

The solution is to not close/reset the encoder if it was initialized but never used (no data written).

